{
 "_doc": "MANAGERS-1 (Rowan, 2026-11-25): \"All of those managers need to own their own code and make sure that all open loops are closed ... operate just like a full-time team\". The platform side of the manager register: for every role file in agents/roles, the code that runs it (`runs`), its clocks (`runtime` is the tick's dispatch beat, roles.last_tick_at, read live by `managers --live`; `clock:<id>` is a clock in doctrine/clocks.json; `workflow:<file>` is a scheduled workflow), a closing probe that is not its own role file (`probe`) and the code that settles what it files (`settles`). Its loops are not listed here: they are the rows of instances/ultrastable/loop-registry.json it owns, and it must own at least one besides role-<id>. Which managers a system runs, and how a deployment switches one off, is seed/autonomy/managers.json (the one register every seeded system carries). `seed_absent` records, with a reason, a platform manager the seed does not carry; `platform_absent` the reverse. Read by foundation/managers.py: `python3 -m foundation managers --check` (in checks), `--report` (the matrix), `--live` (detector managers-live). Ultrastable is the foundation for any autonomous system. `closure` is the reading behind `managers --closure`: a manager with at least min_ended ended items of which more than max_shelved_share ended shelved (closed by the time limit with no fix) files more than it closes. `managers --clocks` lists scheduled manager workflows that are not clocks in doctrine/clocks.json.",
